Tim — great. Confirmed: Dec 12 02:00-04:00 PT and noted you as POC. Below are two minimal patterns you can adopt immediately. Both are in sdk_samples.zip; I pasted small variants so you can see the intent.

1) Node (generation call) — idempotent wrapper using idempotency token + one guarded retry on network failure:

```js
// pseudo-code — Node + fetch
async function generateAndSave(payload, idempotencyKey) {
  const url = 'https://runtime.redwood.ai/v2/generate';
  const headers = {
    'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + process.env.REDWOOD_MIGRATION_KEY,
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
    'Idempotency-Key': idempotencyKey
  };
  // helper for exponential backoff with full jitter
  const backoff = (attempt) => Math.floor(Math.random() * Math.min(200 * 2 ** attempt, 2000));

  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 2; attempt++) {
    try {
      const controller = new AbortController();
      const timeout = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 30000); // overall 30s guard
      const res = await fetch(url, { method: 'POST', headers, body: JSON.stringify(payload), signal: controller.signal });
      clearTimeout(timeout);
      if (res.ok) {
        const body = await res.json();
        // safe to persist — server honors Idempotency-Key for duplicate suppression
        await saveToDB(body);
        return body;
      }
      if (res.status === 429 || res.status >= 500) throw new Error('retryable');
      // non-retryable error — bubble up
      throw new Error('non-retryable');
    } catch (err) {
      if (attempt === 1) throw err;
      const delay = backoff(attempt);
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, delay));
    }
  }
}
```

Notes on the above: Redwood will honor Idempotency-Key for generation endpoints for 24 hours by default. If your workflow needs a longer de-dupe window, we can extend it per account.

2) Python (simple rate-limit aware client) — full-jitter backoff on 429s and metric hooks:

```py
# pseudo-code
import requests, time, random

def call_generation(payload, idempotency_key):
    url = 'https://runtime.redwood.ai/v2/generate'
    headers = {'Authorization': f'Bearer {os.getenv("REDWOOD_MIGRATION_KEY")}', 'Idempotency-Key': idempotency_key}
    max_retries = 4
    for attempt in range(max_retries):
        r = requests.post(url, json=payload, headers=headers, timeout=30)
        if r.status_code == 200:
            return r.json()
        if r.status_code == 429:
            # full jitter: sleep random between 0 and cap
            cap = min(0.2 * (2 ** attempt), 2.0)
            time.sleep(random.random() * cap)
            continue
        if r.status_code >= 500:
            # exponential backoff without jitter for server errors in this example
            time.sleep(0.2 * (2 ** attempt))
            continue
        r.raise_for_status()
    raise Exception('max retries exceeded')
```

Endpoint migration mapping (quick):
- old: https://api.redwood.ai/v1/text/generate -> new: https://runtime.redwood.ai/v2/generate
- old embedding path -> new: https://runtime.redwood.ai/v2/embeddings (note: embeddings are rate-limited separately)
- compatibility header: X-Redwood-Compat: legacy (only while phasing)

Rate-limit operational guidance:
- Use token-bucket smoothing on your client to avoid large bursts. Set a soft queue depth of 50 requests per worker and drain at target RPS.
- Expose a metric that counts 429s and average retry delay; trigger an alert if 429s > 1% or avg retry delay > 500ms for 5m.

